ARTICLES = [
{"slug": "offside-rule-explained", "title": "The Offside Rule Explained Simply", "date": "2026-11-04", "tag": "Rules",
 "summary": "Offside is the rule fans argue about most. Here is what it really means, in plain words, with simple examples.",
 "body": """
<p>Ask ten football fans to explain offside and you might get ten different answers. Yet the rule itself is quite simple once you break it into small pieces. Let's do exactly that.</p>
<h2>The basic idea</h2>
<p>A player is in an <strong>offside position</strong> if, at the moment a teammate plays the ball to them, they are nearer to the opponent's goal line than both the ball and the second-last defender. In most cases the "last defender" is the goalkeeper, so in practice the attacker must not be behind the last outfield defender when the pass is made.</p>
<h2>Being offside is not always an offence</h2>
<p>Here is the part many people miss: simply standing in an offside position is <em>not</em> a foul. The referee only blows the whistle when the player in that position becomes involved in play, for example by:</p>
<ul><li>playing or touching the ball,</li><li>blocking the goalkeeper's view,</li><li>challenging an opponent for the ball, or</li><li>gaining an advantage from a rebound off the post, the crossbar or a defender.</li></ul>
<h2>When you can never be offside</h2>
<ul><li>In your own half of the pitch.</li><li>If you are level with the second-last defender (level is onside).</li><li>Directly from a goal kick, a throw-in or a corner kick.</li></ul>
<h2>Which body parts count?</h2>
<p>Only the parts of the body you can legally score with count: the head, body and feet. Hands and arms do not count, for attackers or defenders. That is why modern offside lines on television are drawn from a shoulder, a knee or a boot, never from a hand.</p>
<h2>Why the rule exists</h2>
<p>Without offside, a team could leave one striker standing next to the opponent's goalkeeper all match, waiting for long balls. The rule forces attackers to time their runs, and it rewards clever movement. Watch a top striker like a hawk and you will see them drifting along the defensive line, waiting for the exact moment to sprint in behind.</p>
<h2>A quick example</h2>
<p>A midfielder passes forward. At that moment the striker is half a step behind the last defender. Flag up: offside. If the striker had stayed level and only started running after the pass was played, it would be a perfectly good goal.</p>
<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> what is the most controversial offside decision you have ever seen? Share it with us on our Facebook Page.</p>
"""},
{"slug": "football-formations-explained", "title": "Football Formations Explained: 4-4-2, 4-3-3 and 3-5-2", "date": "2026-11-04", "tag": "Tactics",
 "summary": "What do those numbers like 4-3-3 actually mean? A simple guide to the most popular football formations.",
 "body": """
<p>When commentators say a team is playing "4-3-3", they are describing how the ten outfield players are arranged. The numbers always go from the back to the front, and the goalkeeper is never counted.</p>
<h2>4-4-2: the classic</h2>
<p>Four defenders, four midfielders and two strikers. It is simple, balanced and easy for players to understand. Each player has a clear partner, and the two strikers can help each other up front. Its weakness is that the middle of the pitch can be outnumbered by teams with three central midfielders.</p>
<h2>4-3-3: control and width</h2>
<p>Four defenders, three central midfielders and three attackers, usually one central striker and two wide forwards. This shape is popular with teams that like to keep the ball, because the three midfielders form a triangle that makes passing easier. The wide forwards stretch the defence and often cut inside to shoot.</p>
<h2>3-5-2: wing-backs everywhere</h2>
<p>Three centre-backs, five midfielders and two strikers. The two widest midfielders are called <strong>wing-backs</strong>: they defend like full-backs and attack like wingers, which makes it one of the most tiring roles in football. The extra centre-back gives security, and the busy midfield helps win the ball back.</p>
<h2>4-2-3-1: the modern favourite</h2>
<p>Two holding midfielders protect the back four, while three attacking midfielders support a lone striker. The player in the middle of the three, the "number 10", is usually the most creative player on the pitch.</p>
<h2>Formations change during the match</h2>
<p>A formation is only a starting point. A team might defend in a 4-4-2 and then attack in a 2-3-5 as full-backs push forward. Coaches now talk about shapes "with the ball" and "without the ball" instead of one fixed formation.</p>
<h2>Which one is best?</h2>
<p>There is no perfect formation. The best one is the shape that fits the players a coach has. A team with fast wingers might love 4-3-3, while a team with strong defenders might prefer three centre-backs.</p>
<p><strong>Question for you:</strong> which formation does your favourite team play, and do you agree with it?</p>
"""},
{"slug": "short-history-of-the-world-cup", "title": "A Short History of the FIFA World Cup", "date": "2026-11-04", "tag": "History",
 "summary": "From 13 teams in Uruguay in 1930 to 48 teams across three countries in 2026: how the World Cup became the biggest show on Earth.",
 "body": """
<p>Every four years, billions of people stop what they are doing to watch the FIFA World Cup. But the tournament started small, almost a century ago.</p>
<h2>1930: where it all began</h2>
<p>The first World Cup was held in <strong>Uruguay in 1930</strong>. Only 13 teams took part, and many European nations stayed at home because the boat trip to South America took weeks. The hosts, Uruguay, beat Argentina 4-2 in the final to become the first world champions.</p>
<h2>Growing bigger</h2>
<p>The tournament was not played in 1942 or 1946 because of the Second World War. After that it grew steadily: 16 teams for many years, then 24 from 1982, and 32 from 1998.</p>
<h2>The giants of the World Cup</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Brazil</strong> have won the most titles, with five.</li><li><strong>Germany</strong> and <strong>Italy</strong> have four each.</li><li><strong>Argentina</strong> have three, the latest in Qatar in 2022.</li><li><strong>France</strong> and <strong>Uruguay</strong> have two each.</li></ul>
<h2>Africa's rise</h2>
<p>African teams have made huge steps. Cameroon reached the quarter-finals in 1990, Senegal in 2002 and Ghana in 2010. In 2022, <strong>Morocco</strong> went even further and became the first African and Arab team to reach the semi-finals.</p>
<h2>2026: a new era</h2>
<p>The 2026 World Cup is the first with <strong>48 teams</strong>, and the first hosted by three countries: the <strong>USA, Canada and Mexico</strong>. More teams means more nations get to dream, and more surprise stories for fans everywhere.</p>
<h2>Why we love it</h2>
<p>The World Cup is more than football. It brings countries together, creates heroes overnight and gives small nations a chance to shock the giants. That magic is why it remains the most watched sporting event on the planet.</p>
<p><strong>Tell us:</strong> what is your favourite World Cup memory?</p>
"""},
{"slug": "morocco-2022-world-cup-run", "title": "Morocco's Historic 2022 World Cup Run", "date": "2026-11-04", "tag": "History",
 "summary": "How the Atlas Lions became the first African and Arab team to reach a World Cup semi-final, step by step.",
 "body": """
<p>Before the 2022 World Cup in Qatar, few people expected Morocco to get out of a group with Croatia and Belgium. A few weeks later, the Atlas Lions were in the semi-finals and the whole world was talking about them.</p>
<h2>Topping a tough group</h2>
<p>Morocco started with a <strong>0-0 draw against Croatia</strong>, the finalists from 2018. Then came a famous <strong>2-0 win over Belgium</strong>, one of the best-ranked teams in the world. A <strong>2-1 win against Canada</strong> made Morocco group winners, ahead of Croatia.</p>
<h2>Beating Spain on penalties</h2>
<p>In the round of 16, Morocco held Spain to a <strong>0-0 draw</strong> after extra time. In the penalty shoot-out, goalkeeper <strong>Yassine Bounou</strong> became a national hero by saving two penalties, and Achraf Hakimi calmly chipped the winning spot-kick.</p>
<h2>Making history against Portugal</h2>
<p>In the quarter-final, <strong>Youssef En-Nesyri</strong> rose high to head in the only goal of the game. Morocco beat Portugal 1-0 and became the <strong>first African and first Arab team ever to reach a World Cup semi-final</strong>.</p>
<h2>The end of the road</h2>
<p>Morocco lost the semi-final 2-0 to France, the defending champions, and then lost the third-place match 2-1 to Croatia. Finishing fourth was still the best result ever by an African nation.</p>
<h2>Why it mattered</h2>
<p>Coach <strong>Walid Regragui</strong> built a team that defended with discipline and fought for every ball. Players celebrating with their mothers on the pitch became one of the most loved images of the tournament. Fans across Africa and the Arab world felt that this success belonged to them too.</p>
<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> who was your favourite Moroccan player at the 2022 World Cup?</p>
"""},
{"slug": "what-is-var", "title": "What Is VAR and How Does It Work?", "date": "2026-11-04", "tag": "Rules",
 "summary": "VAR checks the biggest decisions in a match. Here is when it is used, who is involved and why it still causes arguments.",
 "body": """
<p>VAR stands for <strong>Video Assistant Referee</strong>. It is a team of officials who watch the match on screens and help the referee avoid clear and obvious mistakes. It was used at a World Cup for the first time in Russia in 2018.</p>
<h2>What can VAR check?</h2>
<p>VAR only gets involved in four types of situation:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Goals</strong>: was there a foul, a handball or an offside in the build-up?</li><li><strong>Penalties</strong>: should one be given, or was a penalty given by mistake?</li><li><strong>Direct red cards</strong>: was a serious foul missed or wrongly punished?</li><li><strong>Mistaken identity</strong>: did the referee book or send off the wrong player?</li></ul>
<h2>How a check works</h2>
<p>The VAR team looks at every goal and big moment automatically. If they see a clear error, they tell the referee through the earpiece. The referee can accept their advice or go to the pitch-side monitor for an <strong>on-field review</strong>. When you see the referee drawing a rectangle in the air, that is the signal for a VAR decision.</p>
<h2>Why does it take so long?</h2>
<p>Offside checks need lines drawn on the screen at the exact moment the ball is played, and the officials want to be sure before overturning a decision. Some competitions now use semi-automated offside technology to make these checks faster.</p>
<h2>Why fans still argue</h2>
<p>VAR is meant to fix <em>clear and obvious</em> errors, but people disagree about what "clear" means. A handball one fan thinks is obvious might look accidental to another. Many supporters also miss the old joy of celebrating a goal straight away without waiting for a check.</p>
<h2>Has it made football fairer?</h2>
<p>Most studies and leagues report that more key decisions are now correct. But football is still a game of opinions, and VAR has not ended the debates, it has just moved them to the screen.</p>
<p><strong>What do you think:</strong> has VAR made football better or worse?</p>
"""},
]
